1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
PART 3

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:03,789
Cathy:

3
00:00:03,789 --> 00:00:04,271
OK, Graham,

4
00:00:04,271 --> 00:00:07,650
so let's check we both know what we're supposed to be doing.

5
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:08,374
Graham:

6
00:00:08,374 --> 00:00:08,560
OK.

7
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:09,104
Cathy:

8
00:00:09,104 --> 00:00:13,577
So, for the university's open day, we have to plan a display on British

9
00:00:13,577 --> 00:00:13,940
life

10
00:00:13,940 --> 00:00:16,600
and literature in the mid-19th century.

11
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:16,768
Graham:

12
00:00:16,768 --> 00:00:17,460
That's right.

13
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:20,545
But we'll have some people to help us find the materials

14
00:00:20,545 --> 00:00:21,230
and set it up,

15
00:00:21,230 --> 00:00:24,460
remember–for the moment, we just need to plan it.

16
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:25,180
Cathy:Good.

17
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:29,500
So have you gathered who's expected to come and see the display?

18
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,189
Is it for the people studying English,

19
00:00:32,189 --> 00:00:34,110
or students from other departments?

20
00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:35,600
I'm not clear about it.

21
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:36,600
Graham:Nor me.

22
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:38,930
That was how it used to be,

23
00:00:38,930 --> 00:00:40,927
but it didn't attract many people,

24
00:00:40,927 --> 00:00:41,260
so

25
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:45,898
this year it's going to be part of an open day, to raise the

26
00:00:45,898 --> 00:00:46,560
university's profile.

27
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:50,262
It'll be publicized in the city, to encourage people to come

28
00:00:50,262 --> 00:00:53,290
and find out something of what goes on here.

29
00:00:53,290 --> 00:00:58,234
And it's included in the information that's sent to people who are considering applying

30
00:00:58,234 --> 00:01:00,000
to study here next year.

31
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,610
Cathy:Presumably some current students and lecturers will come?

32
00:01:03,610 --> 00:01:05,172
Graham:I would imagine so,

33
00:01:05,172 --> 00:01:08,920
but we've been told to concentrate on the other categories of people.

34
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:09,623
Cathy:Right.

35
00:01:09,623 --> 00:01:14,190
We don't have to cover the whole range of 19th-century literature, do we?

36
00:01:14,190 --> 00:01:17,318
Graham:No, it's entirely up to us.

37
00:01:17,318 --> 00:01:20,000
I suggest just using Charles Dickens.

38
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:21,474
Cathy:That's a good idea.

39
00:01:21,474 --> 00:01:23,242
Most people have heard of him,

40
00:01:23,242 --> 00:01:25,600
and have probably read some of his novels,

41
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:27,809
or seen films based on them,

42
00:01:27,809 --> 00:01:31,490
so that's a good lead-in to life in his time.

43
00:01:31,490 --> 00:01:32,121
Graham:Exactly.

44
00:01:32,121 --> 00:01:36,539
And his novels show the awful conditions that most people had to live in,

45
00:01:36,539 --> 00:01:37,170
don't they:

46
00:01:37,170 --> 00:01:40,440
he wanted to shock people into doing something about it.

47
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:43,770
Cathy:Did he do any campaigning, other than writing?

48
00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:49,370
Graham:Yes, he campaigned for education and other social reforms,

49
00:01:49,370 --> 00:01:50,261
and gave talks,

50
00:01:50,261 --> 00:01:53,530
but I'm inclined to ignore that and focus on the novels.

51
00:01:53,530 --> 00:01:56,930
Cathy:Yes, I agree.

52
00:01:56,930 --> 00:01:57,873
Cathy:OK,

53
00:01:57,873 --> 00:02:03,530
so now shall we think about a topic linked to each novel?

54
00:02:03,530 --> 00:02:04,179
Graham:Yes.

55
00:02:04,179 --> 00:02:08,720
I've printed out a list of Dickens's novels in the order they were published,

56
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:12,530
in the hope you'd agree to focus on him!

57
00:02:12,530 --> 00:02:14,282
Cathy:You're lucky I did agree!

58
00:02:14,282 --> 00:02:15,450
Let's have a look.

59
00:02:15,450 --> 00:02:23,000
OK, the first was The Pickwick Papers, published in 1836.

60
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,433
It was very successful when it came out, wasn't it,

61
00:02:26,433 --> 00:02:29,180
and was adapted for the theatre straight away.

62
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:31,430
Graham:There's an interesting point, though,

63
00:02:31,430 --> 00:02:34,430
that there's a character who keeps falling asleep,

64
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:39,140
and that medical condition was named after the book – Pickwickian Syndrome.

65
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:39,814
Cathy:Oh,

66
00:02:39,814 --> 00:02:42,849
so why don't we use that as the topic,

67
00:02:42,849 --> 00:02:45,210
and include some quotations from the novel?

68
00:02:45,210 --> 00:02:47,927
Graham:Right.Next is Oliver Twist.

69
00:02:47,927 --> 00:02:51,550
There's a lot in the novel about poverty.

70
00:02:51,550 --> 00:02:54,000
But maybe something less obvious …

71
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,500
Cathy:Well Oliver is taught how to steal, isn't he?

72
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:02,952
We could use that to illustrate the fact that very few children went to

73
00:03:02,952 --> 00:03:03,270
school,

74
00:03:03,270 --> 00:03:05,002
particularly not poor children,

75
00:03:05,002 --> 00:03:07,600
so they learnt in other ways.

76
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:09,340
Graham:Good idea.

77
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:10,500
What's next?

78
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:12,374
Cathy:Maybe Nicholas Nickleby.

79
00:03:12,374 --> 00:03:17,060
Actually he taught in a really cruel school, didn't he?

80
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:18,389
Graham:That's right.

81
00:03:18,389 --> 00:03:23,260
But there's also the company of touring actors that Nicholas joins.

82
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:28,440
We could do something on theaters and other amusements of the time.

83
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:31,260
We don't want only the bad things, do we?

84
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:32,460
Cathy:OK.

85
00:03:32,460 --> 00:03:34,427
Graham:What about Martin Chuzzlewit?

86
00:03:34,427 --> 00:03:37,180
He goes to the USA, doesn't he?

87
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:37,975
Cathy:Yes,

88
00:03:37,975 --> 00:03:41,550
and Dickens himself had been there a year before,

89
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:44,720
and drew on his experience there in the novel.

90
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:49,390
Graham:I wonder, though … The main theme is selfishness,

91
00:03:49,390 --> 00:03:54,000
so we could do something on social justice?

92
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,500
No, too general, let's keep to your idea – I think it would work well.

93
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:02,940
Cathy:He wrote Bleak House next – that's my favourite of his novels.

94
00:04:02,940 --> 00:04:04,420
Graham:Yes, mine too.

95
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:07,690
His satire of the legal system is pretty powerful.

96
00:04:07,690 --> 00:04:08,793
Cathy:That's true,

97
00:04:08,793 --> 00:04:11,000
but think about Esther, the heroine.

98
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,936
As a child she lives with someone she doesn't know is her aunt,

99
00:04:14,936 --> 00:04:16,450
who treats her very badly.

100
00:04:16,450 --> 00:04:19,137
Then she's very happy living with her guardian,

101
00:04:19,137 --> 00:04:22,160
and he puts her in charge of the household.

102
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:25,753
And at the end she gets married and her guardian gives her

103
00:04:25,753 --> 00:04:27,250
and her husband a house,

104
00:04:27,250 --> 00:04:29,010
where of course they're very happy.

105
00:04:29,010 --> 00:04:30,690
Graham:Yes, I like that.

106
00:04:30,690 --> 00:04:33,998
Cathy:What shall we take next?

107
00:04:33,998 --> 00:04:35,100
Little Dorrit?

108
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:38,760
Old Mr Dorrit has been in a debtors' prison for years …

109
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:41,370
Graham:So was Dickens's father, wasn't he?

110
00:04:41,370 --> 00:04:42,610
Cathy:That's right.

111
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:47,250
Graham:What about focusing on the part when Mr Dorrit inherits a fortune,

112
00:04:47,250 --> 00:04:50,410
and he starts pretending he's always been rich?

113
00:04:50,410 --> 00:04:51,630
Cathy:Good idea.

114
00:04:51,630 --> 00:04:52,406
Graham:OK,

115
00:04:52,406 --> 00:04:57,842
so next we need to think about what materials we want to illustrate each

116
00:04:57,842 --> 00:04:58,230
issue.

117
00:04:58,230 --> 00:05:02,000
That's going to be quite hard.
